## prd.md

Follow `.ai/rules/prd.md` and the playbook `.ai/skills/write-prd/SKILL.md`. The template this folder started from shows the exact format.
Read the code the document is about before you write: the criteria must describe what changes relative to what the code does today.

- First lines: `# {{feature}}: <short title>`, then `Status: Draft (generated from {{sourceName}}; a human must review it before implementation)`.
  The title names the **feature**, in two to four words (`Customer Login`, `Password Reset`), not the document (`Requirements`): the spec folder is named after it.
- **Objective**, then **User stories & acceptance criteria**, then **Existing behaviour that must keep working**, Permissions & roles, Data & validation, Contract impact, Out of scope.
- Every acceptance criterion is one line: `- **AC-001** Given ..., when ..., then ... (Source: <page or section of the document>)`.
  - One observable behaviour per criterion. Observable from outside: a response, a page, a command's result, a file.
  - Include the unhappy paths the document implies: validation errors, not found, unauthorized, boundaries (0, 1, max, max+1), empty states.
  - Quote numbers, limits, status codes and wording **exactly as the document states them**.
  - **Never invent requirements.** If a criterion cannot be made testable without filling a gap, follow the conventions you see in the existing code
    and record the gap in the last section as a line starting with `ASSUMPTION:`.
  - Requirements that cannot be verified through a test, a command or a file (visual design, performance targets, process, policy) do **not** become
    criteria: list them under "Out of scope / not machine-verifiable" with their source.
- Finish with a section **Assumptions & open questions**: every assumption, ambiguity or question for the document's author, one per line. Nothing is silently decided.
- No `TODO(prd)` markers anywhere.

## acceptance.json

Format: `{ "feature": "{{feature}}", "checks": [ ... ] }`. Every acceptance criterion needs at least one check; every check lists the criteria it proves in `covers`.
See `.ai/skills/write-fact-checks/SKILL.md`. In short:

```json
{
  "id": "CHK-001",
  "name": "A negative quantity is rejected",
  "covers": ["AC-002"],
  "type": "cmd",
  "run": "<the repository's own command that runs ONE named test; see .ai/context/commands.md>",
  "cwd": ".",
  "expectExit": 0
}
```

- `type` is `cmd` (run a command and check its exit code and output), `file` (a file exists / contains something) or `http` (call the running system; `target` must be one of: {{targets}}).
  If no target is listed, the running app is not available to the checks: use `cmd` and `file` only.
- Prefer a `cmd` check that runs a test **you name**. The developer agent will be told to write a test with exactly that name, so give it a precise, runnable filter.
- Check ids are `CHK-001`, `CHK-002`, ... Do not weaken a check to make it easy: it must fail when the behaviour is wrong.

